<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manuel Chrysoloras</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1350–<a href="/years/1415">1415</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manuel Chrysoloras was a Byzantine scholar and diplomat who taught Greek in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> from 1397, the first sustained teaching of the language in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> for centuries. His pupils included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-bruni">Leonardo Bruni</a> and Guarino da Verona, and his grammar, the Erotemata, was widely used. His teaching opened classical Greek literature to the Italian humanists. He died at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-constance">Council of Constance</a> in 1415.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manuel-chrysoloras.jpg" alt="Manuel Chrysoloras" width="320" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paolo Uccello. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manuel-chrysoloras-paolo-uccello-louvre-15th.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
